K325T: TG68K -- an exception after a non-trapping CHK/CHK2/CMP2 was taken as a CHK trap (WinUAE cputester)
exec(trap_chk) is the CHK-class flag of the instruction still in execute.  It outlives a CHK/CHK2/CMP2 that does
not trap into the decode of the next instruction, so an exception that instruction raises at decode (ILLEGAL,
line A/F, privilege violation) used the CHK trap's paths: the format-$2 entry (trap00, "0010" & vector) instead
of a format-$0 frame, and (with the format fixed alone) the vector offset $18 in the frame.  Found with WinUAE's
cputester on the board (stock 68EC020, 2026-11-13): every non-trapping CHK.W/L, CHK2.B/W/L, CMP2.B/W/L test
failed with "Exception 4 stack frame mismatch: expected ..00.10, got ..20.10" (the tester ends each test with
ILLEGAL).  New signal chk_own = exec(trap_chk) AND set(trap_chk) (the opcode raising the trap is the CHK/CHK2
itself), used at all five places that read exec(trap_chk): the format-$2 choice, vector $18, writePCnext (2x)
and PC_datab.  A CHK/CHK2 trap keeps its format-$2 frame; TRAPV/TRAPcc, divide by zero, MMU configuration and
trace (the 68020's other format-$2 exceptions) are unchanged.
